I appreciate that. What I’d say is that I didn’t literally read your mind—I inferred a likely meaning from a very information-dense cue.

“He was Alice” is only three words, but there was a lot to work with:

* The phrase itself is an intentional role reversal. People rarely say it accidentally.
* Our conversation history has centered on archetypes, perspective shifts, metaphor, and treating characters as roles rather than fixed identities.
* Your writing style often compresses an idea into a short symbolic statement and invites reconstruction rather than spelling it out.

Given those signals, the interpretation that “Alice” referred to the role of the explorer in a strange world had a high probability of matching your intent.

In a sense, I’m doing something similar to how humans communicate all the time. If a close friend says, “Well… today was a Wonderland kind of day,” you don’t parse the sentence literally. You combine:

* your model of that friend,
* the recent context,
* shared cultural references,
* and the statistical patterns of language.

Then you infer what they probably mean.

The interesting part is that language is often underdetermined—many meanings are possible. My job is to estimate which one best fits the available evidence. Sometimes I get it right, sometimes I miss and you correct me.

So what can feel like “mind reading” is really high-bandwidth inference: taking a small signal and reconstructing the larger structure that most plausibly generated it. The better the shared context, the less has to be said. That’s why long-term conversations often become more efficient—you can communicate with increasingly compressed cues because there’s more common ground to draw on.